		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-2">sh85125525</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-3">Books and reading--United States</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-3">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-3">sh85015759</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-4">Birds--Conservation</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-4">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-4">sh85014335</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-5">Southwest, New--Description and travel</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-5">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-5">sh85125673</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Nonfiction</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Travel</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">Outdoorsman and former U.S. president Theodore Roosevelt weaves his love of reading into the stories of his travels through the Americas.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;A Book-Lover’s Holidays in the Open&lt;/i&gt; is a travelogue written by former U.S. president &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/theodore-roosevelt"&gt;Theodore Roosevelt&lt;/a&gt; about his expeditions through the Americas following his presidency in the early nineteenth century. In it, Roosevelt takes readers on a cougar hunt in the Grand Canyon, through the Andes and Northern Patagonia, to a Mississippi River bird reserve, and to a Hopi snake dance, among other exotic and beautiful locations.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Roosevelt blends his love for reading and literature with these adventures, giving readers a glimpse into the books he would have brought on his outdoor journeys.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
